Not every engineer follows the straight line from CS degree to FAANG to staff title. I dropped out of college, came back two years later, and took a path that looks nothing like the "ideal" career trajectory. Here's what I learned along the way.

## The Winding Road

I'm a first-generation college graduate. Nobody in my family had navigated the university system before, and there was no roadmap for "how to become a software engineer." When I first enrolled, I wasn't ready — so I dropped out.

Those two years away weren't wasted. I worked, figured out what I actually wanted to build, and came back with a clarity that most 18-year-olds don't have. When I returned, I treated every class, project, and internship like it mattered — because I'd seen the alternative.

## Starting at Camgian

My first real engineering role was at Camgian, working on sensor data processing and backend systems. It wasn't a Silicon Valley startup or a big tech company. It was a small team in Mississippi solving hard problems with real constraints.

That environment taught me things a larger company never would have:

- **Ownership from day one** — there's no "junior engineer tasks" when your team is small. You ship production code in your first week.
- **Full-stack out of necessity** — I learned Python, databases, deployment, and monitoring because there was nobody else to hand it off to.
- **Problem-first thinking** — we didn't pick technologies because they were trendy. We picked them because they solved the problem in front of us.

## The Jump to Data Infrastructure

Moving to Estuary as a Senior Software Engineer was a step into a fundamentally different scale. Suddenly I was building connectors that handled hundreds of thousands of concurrent streams, working with distributed systems, and shipping code that ran 24/7 in production.

The transition wasn't seamless. Here's what caught me off guard:

```python
# What I thought production async code looked like
async def fetch_all_accounts():
    tasks = [fetch_account(id) for id in account_ids]
    return await asyncio.gather(*tasks)

# What it actually looks like when you have 6,500 accounts
async def fetch_all_accounts():
    semaphore = asyncio.Semaphore(50)  # Don't melt the API
    async def _bounded_fetch(id):
        async with semaphore:
            for attempt in range(MAX_RETRIES):
                try:
                    return await fetch_account(id)
                except RateLimitError:
                    await asyncio.sleep(2 ** attempt)
                except Exception:
                    if attempt == MAX_RETRIES - 1:
                        raise
                    await asyncio.sleep(1)
    tasks = [_bounded_fetch(id) for id in account_ids]
    return await asyncio.gather(*tasks, return_exceptions=True)
```

The difference between "works on my machine" and "works at scale in production" is entirely about handling the unhappy path.

## What the Non-Linear Path Gives You

Looking back, every detour added something:

**Dropping out taught me urgency.** When you come back to school after seeing what life looks like without a degree, you don't waste time. Every project is an opportunity, not an obligation.

**Small-company experience taught me breadth.** At Camgian, I couldn't specialize. I had to understand the full stack, from sensor protocols to REST APIs to deployment pipelines. That breadth is exactly what makes a senior engineer effective — you can debug across boundaries.

**Working on data infrastructure taught me depth.** Building connectors that handle 500K+ concurrent streams forced me to understand async programming, state management, and distributed systems at a level that no tutorial covers.

## Advice for Engineers on a Non-Linear Path

If your career doesn't look like the "standard" path, here's what I'd tell you:

**1. Your background is an asset, not a liability.** First-gen engineers, career changers, and self-taught developers bring perspectives that homogeneous teams lack. The ability to explain complex systems in plain language — something you develop when nobody around you speaks "tech" — is incredibly valuable.

**2. Small companies are underrated.** You'll learn more in two years at a 20-person company than in five years at a company where your blast radius is a single microservice. The breadth of experience compounds.

**3. Side projects matter more than credentials.** Nobody has ever asked me about my GPA. They ask about what I've built, what I've shipped, and what I've learned. My side projects — including [this very website](https://justinsmith.sh) — have opened more doors than any certification.

**4. Find your craft outside of code.** I play disc golf competitively. It has nothing to do with software engineering, but the mental model — reading the terrain, managing risk, staying composed under pressure — translates directly. Having interests outside of tech makes you a more creative problem solver.

## Where I Am Now

Today I'm building production data infrastructure at Estuary, shipping connectors that power real-time data pipelines for companies that need every event captured without fail. The path here was anything but straight, and I wouldn't change it.

The non-linear path isn't a detour. It's a different route to the same destination — and sometimes, a better one.

